//! The clock key `rom`, the default: the kernel writes no clock register and
//! the part runs at whatever the mask ROM left.
//!
//! One of the `s3clock` modules, mapped by a case's `clock` (the runner) or an
//! image's `[keys]` (`sosimage`). The HAL re-exports its `clock_init`, which
//! `kmain` calls first (`../../kernel/lib.saw`; `../../ABI.md` §14).

/// Set the CPU clock this image is built for: the one the ROM left, so
/// nothing is written.
public func clock_init() unsafe { }

/// The clock words this key leaves on the part, as the PSRAM bring-up's S0.3
/// compares them: SYSTEM_SYSCLK_CONF & 0x80FFF, SYSTEM_CPU_PER_CONF & 7 and
/// SPI_MEM_CORE_CLK_SEL & 3, from the part's `sysclk` 0x000a8001, `cpu_per`
/// 0x0000000c and `mspi_core` 0 (../../ABI.md §14h, §16b).
public static PART_SYSCLK: UInt32 = 0x0008_0001
public static PART_CPU_PER: UInt32 = 0x4
public static PART_CORE_CLK_SEL: UInt32 = 0

/// The memory-SPI core clock this key leaves, in MHz: the CPU's own clock, the
/// crystal over the pre-divider of 2 (../../ABI.md §16b).
public static MSPI_CORE_MHZ: UInt = 20
